# Abstract storage layer: design doc

## 1. Goal

Move blog folder storage (`.folder`) and generated-asset storage (`.assets`)
off EBS onto S3, for cost/scalability/deployment reasons, and enforce
per-blog storage quotas — with no added read-path latency, paying any cost
at sync/build time instead.

- **Done:** `.assets` is in S3 and nowhere else (Phase 1), and so are
  blog folders, with a Redis metadata index and a per-file content cache on
  the instance SSD (Phase 2,
  [#2258](https://github.com/blotcms/blot/issues/2258)). Git client repos
  are in S3 too (Phase 3, [#2259](https://github.com/blotcms/blot/issues/2259)).
- **Long term:** node containers scale horizontally with no EBS mount
  (one EBS volume can't be attached to several containers). Each host's
  ephemeral SSD is a cache, never the source of truth.

## 2. Current state

- Neither blog folders nor generated assets are on disk: both are in the
  storage bucket (`config.storage`). There is no `blog_folder_dir` and no
  `data/blogs`.
- A blog's folder is the objects under `{blogID}/folder/`, reached only
  through `app/storage/folder` (§8). `scripts/tests/ci/check-folder-access.js`
  fails CI for anything which names `blog_folder_dir`, `helper/localPath`,
  `data/blogs` or a spec's `this.blogDirectory`.
- Per-blog asset IO all goes through `app/storage/assets.js`: `path`, `url`,
  `commit`, `write`, `writeFrom`, `read`, `exists`, `list`, `walk`,
  `createReadStream`, `ensureLocal`, `serve`, `remove`, `removeAll`, and a
  `NotFoundError`. The assets are in S3 and nowhere else (Phase 1); `path`
  returns a transient staging path under the tmp directory, not a place they
  live. Subdirectories in use: `_thumbnails`, `_image_cache`, `_assets`,
  `_bookmark_screenshots`, `_avatars`, `_template_assets` (see §3's table for
  which are regenerable).
- Blog folders and generated assets map to the public CDN URL as bare
  `{blogID}/{path}`, no scope prefix — relevant to the object-key plan in §3.
- Mostly plain `fs-extra` (`readFile`, `outputFile`, `remove`, `ensureDir`,
  `copy`) plus `express.static()` for serving, with a few exceptions that
  assume a real filesystem: pandoc, sharp and exiftool take file paths;
  iCloud renames whole directory trees to resolve case
  conflicts; the local client (development only, never for users) used to
  watch the folder with chokidar and now watches a source directory of its
  own. (Google Drive's fingerprint read `dev`, `ino`, `ctimeNs` and `mtimeNs`
  until its records moved to storage/folder's size, modified time and SHA-1.
  The git client kept a live `.git` clone in the blog folder and ran `reset
  --hard` there until it moved to the git store, #2259.)
- Rendered template views are cached in-process
  (`app/models/template/fullViewCache.js`, an LRU bounded by count and bytes,
  not Redis), and rendered template CSS/JS in Redis (`app/cdn/index.js`
  `/template`), so neither is read from disk per request.
- Generated assets (thumbnails, doc conversions, image optimization) are
  built asynchronously at sync/build time and written before any visitor
  requests them — the "pay the cost at build time" model already works for
  this half of storage.
- Per-request disk reads remain (§4c): `app/blog/render/retrieve/folder.js`'s
  uncached `fs.readdir` + `fs.stat`, and `app/blog/routes/assets.js` and the
  CDN's `/folder/v-:version` serving folder files. Links in posts are baked at build time by
  `build/plugins/folderAssets` and links in templates by the template's CDN
  manifest, so rendering no longer looks files up.
- The per-blog sync lock is a Redis mutex (`app/sync/lock.js`:
  token-checked `SET NX PX` with a heartbeat), so it works across multiple
  app hosts as is.
- No quota enforcement exists. `models/file` keeps per-blog byte and file
  totals, which the manual audit script
  (`scripts/user/identify-overdue-users.js`, for disabled accounts) adds to
  the sum of the blog's asset object sizes.
- The AWS SDK (`@aws-sdk/client-s3`) is used only by `app/storage/s3.js`.
- Don't store bulk file content in Redis; Redis is
  fine for small bounded metadata (locks, directory listings, cache
  entries).

## 3. Decisions

- **Location: `app/storage`**, not `app/models/storage`. `app/models` is for
  structured domain entities with identity; a blob store is bytes-in/out
  keyed by path. `app/storage` sits alongside `app/models`, `app/helper`,
  `app/clients`, `app/build` as its own top-level concern.
- **Interface: plain functions per scope** (`storage/assets`, later
  `storage/folder`) taking `blogID` first, e.g.
  `assets.path(blogID, "_thumbnails", name)`. No classes — matches the rest
  of `app/`. The API grows only as call sites need it.
- **Naming: a small custom vocabulary** — `read`/`write`/`delete`/`list`/
  `exists`/`size`, not `fs-extra`'s names. `fs-extra` supports operations
  that don't map onto S3 (symlinks, `chmod`, atomic rename); reusing its
  names would invite callers to assume that surface exists.
- **One bucket** (`config.storage.bucket`), not two, with everything for a
  blog under its own prefix:
  - `{blogID}/_thumbnails/...`, `{blogID}/_image_cache/...` etc.: the
    generated assets (`.assets`). Keys are identical to their public CDN paths
    (`cdn.blot.im/{blogID}/...`).
  - `{blogID}/folder/{path}`: the blog's folder content (`.folder`, Phase 2).
    It is public like the rest of `blog_*`, since the blog's own domain
    already serves these files; the exception is `.git` directories inside a
    folder, which the bucket policy denies to everyone outside the AWS
    account (`config/storage-bucket/README.md`).
  - `{blogID}/git/...`: Git client repos (Phase 3), denied the same way.

  **The assets scope is exactly a blog's top-level directories whose names
  start with `_`.** `storage/assets` enforces it: a relPath whose first
  segment doesn't start with `_` is rejected (a CDN request for
  `/{blogID}/folder/...` is a 404), and the whole-scope operations (`walk`,
  `list` of the blog root, `removeAll`, the backfill and its `--verify`) only
  look at the `{blogID}/_*/` prefixes, never at anything else under
  `{blogID}/`. Nothing may be written at a blog's top level without an
  underscore. Blog IDs are always `blog_<uuid>`; `folder/` (Phase 2) and
  `git/` (Phase 3) are the other prefixes under `{blogID}/`.

  Storage class is set per object at write time rather than via
  prefix-filtered lifecycle rules. The whole bucket is public-read for
  `blog_*` keys (no listing, no public writes) because both scopes are
  public by design (except `.git` directories in a folder and
  `{blogID}/git/`, which the policy denies outside the AWS account): assets are
  served on the CDN, and a blog's folder is served at the blog's domain and
  through the CDN's `/folder/v-:version` route. Folder content goes in the same bucket: a second, versioned bucket
  for folders (for a "restore deleted file" feature) was considered and
  turned down.
- **Versioning: off**, bucket-wide. The B2 backup is the only history.
- **Backup: hourly `aws s3 sync` (whole bucket, no `--delete`) to
  Backblaze B2**, US West region (closest to the app's `us-west-2`; B2's
  region is a permanent per-account choice). No-`--delete` plus B2's
  always-on versioning gives grow-and-expire semantics: a B2-native
  lifecycle rule (`daysFromHidingToDeleting`, retention window TBD, see §7)
  cleans up superseded versions without ever expiring unchanged current
  objects. B2 doesn't take S3's `NoncurrentVersionExpiration`; configure
  the rule through the B2 native API or console. Decided over a second AWS account or Bunny Storage, for genuine
  vendor independence (a second cloud problem shouldn't be able to take out
  both the primary and the backup). Projected ~80% reduction in combined
  storage + backup spend versus current EBS; figures deliberately kept out
  of this doc.
- **Code against the S3 API subset B2 also supports**, so B2 can become
  the primary store later. What that rules out, and other B2 differences:
  - No object tagging, storage classes or IAM roles (hosts would hold a
    static B2 application key).
  - Versioning is always on: a delete only hides the object, so a
    lifecycle rule is needed for deletes to free space.
  - Lifecycle rules are B2-native (`daysFromHidingToDeleting`), not S3's.
  - Conditional writes (`If-Match`/`If-None-Match`) aren't documented;
    don't depend on them. The per-blog Redis lock already gives a single
    writer per blog.
  - B2 is outside AWS, so reads from `us-west-2` cross the public
    internet; Backblaze's own benchmarks put S3 ahead on time to first
    byte. Measure from EC2 before making B2 primary.
  - In B2's favour: egress is free up to 3x stored bytes and free to Bunny
    without limit, and its native listing call returns per-file
    `fileInfo`, which S3's `ListObjectsV2` doesn't.
- **No S3 Express One Zone** — single-AZ durability is a bad trade for data
  that already has a reduced backup posture, and its latency benefit
  targets the same problem an SSD cache (if built, §5) would solve.
- **Single region.** Future multi-region compute reads across the network
  from one bucket; live multi-region storage replication would add
  cross-region conflict-resolution risk the current single-writer-per-blog
  lock isn't built to handle.
- **One IAM user for the app**, scoped to the storage bucket and not to
  prefixes — revisit only if a concrete need for narrower blast radius shows
  up.
- **Credentials: a static IAM user key, not the host's instance profile.** The
  app containers use a key scoped to the storage bucket
  (`BLOT_AWS_KEY`/`BLOT_AWS_SECRET`). Reaching instance credentials from a
  container on Docker's bridge network needs the metadata service's hop limit
  raised to 2, and the app host keeps it at 1 so
  a server-side request forgery in the app can't read role credentials, which
  here could write and delete the public bucket. A scoped key in the host's
  env file grants the same access without that exposure, and B2 (if it
  becomes primary) has no roles anyway. Jobs that run on the host itself (e.g.
  the Redis backups, a future B2 sync) can use an instance profile. The SDK
  falls back to the default provider chain when the key isn't set, so this is
  a deployment choice, not code.
- **Separate bucket per environment** (dev/staging/prod).
- **Dev/test: MinIO** in the dev docker-compose stack (with a one-shot job
  that creates the bucket and sets the same public-read-on-`blog_*` policy as
  production), and in the tests: `scripts/tests/invoke.sh` and every CI test
  shard start one, and the test runner creates the bucket
  (`scripts/tests/util/bucket.js`). Dev mirrors production as closely as it
  can, so the dev proxy's `cdn` host serves `/blog_*` from it. The app
  refuses to start without a bucket. Blog folders are in it too, in
  development and specs as in production. MinIO has stopped publishing its own images,
  so these use a pinned third-party build of the upstream release; swap it if
  that becomes a problem. Latency isn't simulated for S3 (toxiproxy only
  fronts Redis).
- **Bunny pulls `blog_*` from the bucket.** Once the bucket is complete, an
  edge rule on the CDN's hostnames (URL matches `*://cdn.blot.im/blog_*` and
  the same for `cdn.blot.site` and `blot-cdn.b-cdn.net`; anchored on the host
  so `/folder/v-.../blog_...` and `/template/...` stay on the app) changes the
  origin to the bucket, and a second sets `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`
  (the app's CORS header no longer applies). The pull zone's forced `Host`
  header may break the bucket origin; `config/storage-bucket/README.md` has
  the rules, the test and the fallback. The app stays the origin for
  everything else on the CDN (`/template`, `/folder`, ...) and still serves
  `/blog_*` itself, from S3, to blog domains and to anything that reaches it
  before the CDN does. The openresty `cdn.` host no longer serves any
  per-blog file from disk. This replaces the earlier plan of keeping the app
  as Bunny's origin and warming Perma-Cache with a GET after each write.
- **The bucket is public for `s3:GetObject` on `blog_*` keys only** — never
  `ListBucket`, never write. A blog's folder content is under
  `{blogID}/folder/`, public like the rest of `blog_*` because the blog's own
  domain already serves it; `.git` directories in a folder and
  `{blogID}/git/` are denied outside the AWS account. Asset
  keys are `{blogID}/{path}`, identical to the CDN path, which is what lets
  Bunny fetch them with no rewriting. `config/storage-bucket`
  has the setup script and the operator steps.
- **S3 is the only store; local disk is only a staging area.** A tool which
  needs a local path (sharp, pandoc, puppeteer) writes to `path(blogID,
  relPath)`, a path under `{tmp}/storage-assets-staging/{blogID}/` on the
  instance store. `commit()` uploads what is there and deletes exactly the
  files it uploaded (a directory such as odt's shared `_assets/media` may hold
  another document's files, which stay until their own commit), then the
  directories it emptied, except a blog's top-level ones, which every build
  of that blog shares. A failed upload deletes the staged files and throws.
  The tmp prune (`app/scheduler/prune-tmp.js`) removes staged files a week
  after they were last written, judging each file rather than its directory,
  far longer than any build. Reads, listing, walking and serving go to S3;
  the files downloaded for `ensureLocal` are a cache in `{tmp}/storage-assets/`.
- **Failure policy.** An upload or delete which fails is thrown to the caller:
  a build fails, a dashboard upload shows an error. There is no local copy to
  fall back on, so nothing is logged and swallowed. That includes blog
  deletion (`removeAll`): the bucket is public for `blog_*` keys, so a delete
  that silently failed would leave a deleted blog's assets readable.
- **`.assets` holds non-regenerable dashboard uploads**, not just
  generated files:

  | Subdir | Written by | Regenerable? |
  |---|---|---|
  | `_thumbnails` | `app/build/thumbnail/create.js` | Yes, from `.folder` |
  | `_image_cache` | `app/build/plugins/image/optimize.js` | Yes, from `.folder` |
  | `_assets` | document converters | Yes, from `.folder` |
  | `_bookmark_screenshots` | `app/build/plugins/linkScreenshot` | Not faithfully — source page may have changed |
  | `_avatars` | `app/dashboard/site/save/avatar.js` | No — exists only in Blot |
  | `_template_assets` | template editor uploads | No — exists only in Blot |

  Nothing may wipe the whole `.assets` scope except blog deletion (today
  `app/sync/rebuild.js` only prefix-deletes `_thumbnails`/`_image_cache`
  with `assets.remove`, and the storage layer shouldn't expose a scope-wide
  delete). Even then
  `removeAll` deletes only the `{blogID}/_*/` prefixes; blog deletion will
  remove the folder scope separately once it exists. Quota is
  enforced on `.folder` writes only; `.assets` bytes are tracked but not
  capped.

## 4. Caveats for moving `.folder` to S3

**a. File timestamps.** S3 and B2 both set `Last-Modified` themselves at
upload time and can't be told otherwise, and neither has ctime or atime.
Custom metadata (`x-amz-meta-mtime`) could hold a chosen mtime, but only a
rewrite changes it and S3's `ListObjectsV2` doesn't return it, so every
listing would need a HEAD per file. Blot doesn't need one: nothing sets an
mtime on blog storage or reads ctime or atime, and every mtime read works
with `Last-Modified`.

- `entry.updated` moves only when the entry's `contentHash` changes, so a
  reconnect, a full resync or the copy to S3 leaves it alone.
- CDN version tokens hash content (`helper/contentVersion`, SHA-1 through
  `helper/transformer/hash`; on S3, probably the index's hash). Large files (the `routes/assets.js` redirect to the CDN) use size+mtime, which
  still changes whenever the file is rewritten.
- Google Drive compares Drive's `modifiedTime` with an "applied" record in
  its client DB. iCloud and Dropbox compare content or provider hashes.
- Google Drive's records of the file Blot holds (`util/localState.js`) are
  storage/folder's `size` and `modified` (rounded to the millisecond, as
  `models/file` keeps it),
  plus the SHA-1 when it's known, checked only when those two moved. The
  older fingerprint (`util/contentFingerprint.js`, `localFingerprint.js`:
  size and nanosecond mtime on disk) is still honoured in records written
  before, and replaced the first time it matches.
- The other mtime reads are display (`retrieve/folder.js`,
  `dashboard/site/folder/stat.js`, `build/multiple.js`) and iCloud
  forwarding the time to the macserver.
- The remaining `utimes` calls don't touch blog storage: the macserver
  sets them on the real iCloud Drive file, and the importer on its temp
  output, which the user downloads as a zip.

Two fallbacks still read the local mtime. Clear both on local disk before
the copy to S3:

- An entry with no stored `contentHash` takes the mtime once to set
  `updated`. If that happens on S3, the post shows the migration date.
  `scripts/entry/backfill-content-hash.js --apply` stores a hash without
  touching `updated`.
- A Drive file with no applied record compares against the local mtime;
  on S3 it would be re-downloaded once.
  `scripts/google-drive/backfill-applied-modified-times.js --apply`
  runs a full (non-resetting) sync for the Drive blogs that have them.
  Old md5 files deferred by sync's verification budget can stay
  unrecorded; they're compared by checksum.

The storage layer's `stat()` returns `size`, `modified` (local mtime now,
the upload time on S3) and `hash`, with no ctime, no atime and no way to set
`modified`. `hash` is planned to be Dropbox's `content_hash` algorithm
(`helper/hashFile`), computed as a file is written and kept in the Redis
index, so it never depends on S3 or B2 ETags; Google Drive blogs also keep an
MD5. Entry `contentHash` is not built from it today: `app/build/index.js`
hashes each source with `helper/transformer/hash` (SHA-1 of the file) and
`contentHash` is a SHA-1 over `path:sha1|path:sha1`. See §7. Accepted
trade-offs:

- `updated` means when Blot received a change, not when the user saved it.
- Non-post files in folder listings show the migration date, once.

**b. Sync clients need a staging pattern, not direct S3 calls.** Google
Drive streams to a temp file then moves it into place; Dropbox's
`delta.js` does recursive `readdir` diffs; iCloud's `resolveCaseConflicts.js`
resolves case conflicts via rename. These all assume a real local
filesystem underneath and need a stage-locally-then-commit pattern against
the storage layer, not raw S3 calls threaded through each client.

**c. Request-time folder reads.**
`app/blog/routes/assets.js` serves from the blog folder with case-folding
and index.html/.html fallbacks; `app/cdn/index.js`'s `/folder/v-:version`
route uses `express.static()` plus `assertNoSymlinks`; `retrieve/folder.js`
still does an uncached
`readdir`+`stat` per request. All of these move onto the storage layer
before `.folder` moves. Metadata (existence, case, fallbacks, listings)
comes from the Redis index, so none of them waits on an S3 listing. Bytes
come from a per-file content cache on the instance SSD, keyed by content
hash so it can't go stale, LRU-bounded (about 50 GB per host to start;
working precedent: `app/clients/icloud/macserver/watcher/monitorDiskUsage.js`).
A miss fetches the one object from S3. Sampled shadow reads during the
dual-write phase measure the latency and tune the cache size.

**d. `app/sync/rebuild.js` processes files serially**
(`async.eachSeries`) — a full rebuild of a large blog would turn into
a long run of serial S3 round trips. Needs bounded concurrency (e.g.
`async.eachLimit`) before `.folder` goes to S3, but `Entry.set` is not yet
safe for two paths of one blog at once: the URL claim in
`models/entry/_setUrl.js`, the blog menu in `models/entry/_assign.js` and
backlinks in `models/entry/set.js` are all read-then-write. Those need
fixing first.

**e. About 40 `err.code === "ENOENT"` checks exist in `app/`**, in about
45 files. The storage
layer needs one backend-agnostic not-found signal (e.g. a `NotFoundError`
class) that means the same thing on local disk (`ENOENT`) and S3
(`404`/`NoSuchKey`).

**f. Symlink checks become moot on S3** — `assertNoSymlinks` has nothing to
check once the backend has no filesystem.

**g. Case sensitivity.** S3 keys are case-sensitive, which removes a
filesystem quirk rather than adding one. Case-insensitive resolution
(today `helper/caseSensitivePath.js`, used by `folderFile.js`, `routes/assets.js`,
the wikilinks plugin, `extractBibAndCSL`, `helper/transformer` and the
Dropbox client)
should consolidate into one storage-layer method, exposed explicitly
(`store.folder.resolveCaseInsensitive(path)`) rather than built into
`.read()`'s hot path — it's specifically a user-authored-link problem
(`![](image.JPG)` vs. `image.jpg`), not a general storage concern, and
`.assets` paths are always Blot-generated so it doesn't apply there.

## 5. Phases

- **Phase 0 (done):** `app/storage/assets.js` is the only module that
  touches generated assets. Callers name a file by `(blogID, relPath)`,
  relative to the blog's asset directory; every `relPath` goes through the
  same escape guard as `path()`. The API: `url`, `write`, `writeFrom` (copy
  or move a local file in), `read`, `exists`, `list`, `walk` (every file in a
  blog's scope, for export and backfill), `createReadStream`, `ensureLocal`
  (an absolute local path for callers which need a real file), `serve` (sends
  the response, keeping range, ETag and Last-Modified) and `remove`. Missing
  files surface as one `NotFoundError` (code `ENOENT`), which S3's
  404/`NoSuchKey` maps to. A few sites hand a path to a tool that writes
  itself (sharp's `.toFile`, pandoc `--extract-media`, puppeteer screenshots,
  the template image and favicon generators), so the shape there is "write to
  `path(blogID, relPath)`, then `commit(blogID, relPath)`" (a file or a
  directory). `removeAll(blogID)` is the only whole-scope delete, used solely
  by blog deletion; it removes the `_*` directories only.
- **Phase 1 (done) — `.assets` first.** These files are written
  asynchronously, UUID- or content-hash-named, and never change once written
  — the simplest shape to move. No production code reads their mtime or
  ctime. Because Bunny fetches them straight from the bucket (§3), each
  object is uploaded with the `Content-Type` its name implies and
  `Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable`, the headers both
  serving routes send; nothing else is stored as metadata.
  - `app/storage/s3.js` (the client and `upload`, `head`, `open`, list and
    delete helpers, over the S3 API subset B2 also supports) sits behind
    `app/storage/assets.js`. `commit()` uploads a file or every file under a
    directory (bounded concurrency); `remove`/`removeAll` delete the key and
    the prefix below it (list + `DeleteObjects`, which also makes
    `rebuild.js`'s `_thumbnails`/`_image_cache` wipe a prefix delete).
  - It moved in stages: writes to disk and S3 together, a backfill of
    existing files, reads from S3 first with the CDN pointed at the bucket,
    then S3 alone and no `data/static` (and no `static` mount in the proxy).
    `scripts/storage/backfill-assets.js --source <dir>` remains for re-seeding
    the bucket from a local copy, such as a backup;
    `config/storage-bucket` creates the bucket and IAM user.
  - The B2 backup of the bucket (§3) is what stands between `_avatars` /
    `_template_assets` and loss, since nothing is kept on the host.
  - Not done: any read cache beyond the `ensureLocal` download cache (it is
    pruned with the rest of the tmp directory); a read cache on ephemeral SSD
    would be bounded in size.
- **Phase 2 — `.folder`** ([#2258](https://github.com/blotcms/blot/issues/2258)
  has the plan, the checklists and the performance gates). S3 becomes the
  source of truth for folder content, under `{blogID}/folder/{path}` in the
  same bucket (§3), outside the assets scope; blog deletion removes it
  separately from `removeAll`. Each phase ships on its own and is reversible.
  - Design in brief: every folder read and write goes through
    `app/storage/folder`; metadata lives in a Redis index (type, size,
    modified, hash per file or directory, pageable per directory, with a
    lowercased lookup), derived from S3, rebuildable from a listing, and
    updated after the S3 write in one `multi()` under the folder lock so it
    is never ahead of S3; bytes are cached per file on the instance SSD (§4c),
    so a blog folder can be larger than the SSD; writes finish in S3 before
    they return; empty directories are zero-byte `{dir}/` marker objects.
  - How it moved (each step shipped on its own and was reversible): A,
    groundwork on disk (one hash per changed file per build, bounded
    rebuild concurrency, cheaper misses in `blog/routes/assets.js`, a scan
    of existing file names); B, every call site onto `app/storage/folder`
    with a local-disk backend, enforced by `check-folder-access.js`; C, an
    S3 backend, the index and the content cache, with disk authoritative: a
    mirror of every write, a backfill, an hourly and daily reconcile and
    sampled shadow reads, soaked in production; D, S3 authoritative blog by
    blog behind `BLOT_FOLDER_PRIMARY`, with a disk copy kept for rollback
    and reverse shadow reads, ramped to every blog (the cutover, #2481); E,
    the disk removed: the local backend, the mirror, the disk copy, shadow
    reads, the reconcile and the flags went in the cleanup PR, and
    `data/blogs` was deleted after it. The B2 offsite sync of the whole
    bucket replaced the data volume's EBS snapshots as the backup.
- **Phase 3 — git client repos.** Done in
  [#2259](https://github.com/blotcms/blot/issues/2259). Every git blog's
  repository is in a walgit-style store (`app/clients/git/store`):
  immutable packs plus a small manifest under `{blogID}/git/`, kept private
  by the bucket policy, with the SSD caching refs, pack indexes and
  commits/trees, and blobs fetched on demand, so a repo can be larger than
  the SSD. No whole-repo archive and no S3 filesystem mount. A push is
  acknowledged once its pack is in S3, under the per-blog lock, and the blog
  folder is materialized from the repo through `storage/folder`, with no
  live `.git` in it. The bare repositories that were in
  `{data_directory}/git/` are deleted from the host once the daily verifier
  has run clean.
- **Quotas** — the Phase 2 index keeps per-blog byte and file totals, which
  enforcement can read. Until then, `scripts/user/identify-overdue-users.js`
  is the manual audit.

## 6. Directory listing

When `.folder` gets a listing primitive (Phase 2), make it a paginated
`.list()` from the start — an unpaginated `readdir()`-equivalent would only
mean migrating every call site off it again later.

```js
const { entries, cursor } = await store.folder.list(path, { cursor, limit });
const allEntries = await store.folder.listAll(path); // buffers everything; for callers that can afford it
```

The listing comes from the Redis index, one directory at a time
(a page is a `ZRANGEBYLEX` of the directory's names set, see
`app/models/file/README`), not from `ListObjectsV2`. `.listAll()` is for callers like `buildFromFolder`'s directory scan
or the quota-reconciliation script that genuinely need everything; the
`folder` template local — the one place a huge directory has actually
caused problems — should call `.list()` directly, never `.listAll()`.

`.list()` is sorted by name (UTF-16 code-unit order), and
its cursor is the last name of the previous page.

## 7. Open questions

- B2 `daysFromHidingToDeleting` retention window (60? 90 days?).
- Whether B2 becomes the primary store rather than only the backup —
  decide after measuring time to first byte from `us-west-2`.
- **What the index hash is, given entry `contentHash` (Phase C).** The plan
  is a Dropbox `content_hash` in the index, so the build and Dropbox can
  compare without reading the file. But entry `contentHash` is SHA-1 based
  (§4a), so an index of Dropbox hashes cannot feed `hashEntrySource`
  directly without changing every stored `contentHash`. Two options, not yet
  chosen:
  - Keep a SHA-1 alongside the Dropbox hash in the index, computed in the
    same pass as the upload. `contentHash` and the CDN version tokens stay
    as they are; the index costs more memory per file.
  - Move `contentHash` to the Dropbox hash, with a conversion in the style of
    `scripts/entry/backfill-content-hash.js` which rewrites each stored
    `contentHash` without moving `updated`. One hash per file, but a
    conversion across every entry that must complete before anything compares
    the new values, and the CDN version tokens would still need a decision.

## 8. `app/storage/folder`

`app/storage/folder` exists, with plain functions taking `blogID` first like
`storage/assets`: `read`, `createReadStream`, `serve`, `write`, `writeFrom`,
`path` + `commit`, `ensureLocal`, `mkdir`, `rmdir`, `remove`, `move`, `stat`,
`exists`, `list`, `listNames`, `listAll`, `walk`, `resolveCaseInsensitive` and `removeAll`.
`index.js` is the public surface and documents each function, and sends
every call to `s3.js` (below). It is the only backend: the local-disk one
went with the cleanup of #2258, and development and specs use MinIO.

`s3.js` is the S3 backend: objects at
`{blogID}/folder/{path}`, a zero-byte marker `{blogID}/folder/{dir}/` for a
directory with nothing in it, and every metadata read (`stat`, `list`,
`walk`, `resolveCaseInsensitive`, ...) from the Redis index `models/file`
(see its README). Writes go to S3 first and the index after, with the SHA-1
computed as the bytes upload; removes delete from S3 first. `move` is copy
plus delete. `path` returns a staging path under the tmp directory which
`commit` uploads. Bytes are read through the content cache (`cache.js`,
below). `app/storage/tests/folder.js` is the contract, on MinIO.
`scripts/storage/rebuild-file-index.js` checks a blog's index against a
listing of the bucket, and with `--apply --live` rebuilds it from that
listing: the listing is read first, but reads during the Redis writes see a
partial folder, so it is for repair, not routine use.

The content cache (`cache.js`) keeps the bytes the S3 backend has read on
the instance store, under `config.folder_cache.directory` (by default
`{tmp}/storage-folder-cache`, which the tmp pruner leaves alone). An entry is
`blogs/{blogID}/{id}/{name}`, where `id` is the SHA-1 of the path and the
file's version (its SHA-1 from the index, or size and modified time where the
index has none), so a changed file has a new key and a copy is never stale.
Downloads go to `tmp/` in the cache, are checked against the index's size and
SHA-1, and are renamed into place, so nothing ever sees a partial or wrong
file; concurrent gets of one entry in a process share a download. It is an
LRU bounded by `max_bytes`, with recency kept as each entry's mtime, so the
order survives a restart: the directory is rescanned at startup and every few
minutes, which is also how processes sharing the directory see each other's
entries (the cap is a soft one between scans). The same check evicts more
when the disk has under `min_free_percent` free. Nothing is evicted while
pinned in this process (`use`) or within a minute of its last use in any
process, which is what keeps a path from `ensureLocal` alive while a tool
opens it. For a tool which runs longer, `withLocal(blogID, path, fn)` calls
`fn(localPath)` with the entry pinned until `fn` settles. The pin is a heartbeat: while held, the entry's
mtime is set again every third of the grace period, and eviction re-checks
the mtime on disk just before deleting, so every process sees a held entry as
recent with no pin files and nothing for a crash to leave behind; a holder
which dies stops touching and the entry is evictable a minute later. Pinned
entries are skipped by eviction and a fill is never refused, so the cache can
run over `max_bytes` for as long as tools hold paths; the next scan after the
release brings it back. The converters (docx, odt, img), the transformer's
folder lookups and the Dropbox uploads use `withLocal`; callers that use the
path at once keep `ensureLocal`. `ensureLocal` caches any size; `read`, `createReadStream` and
`serve` go through the cache for files up to `max_file_bytes` and stream
bigger ones from S3. Writes fill it too (write-through): once `write`,
`writeFrom` or `commit` (which goes through `writeFrom`) has recorded a file
in the index, the bytes it still holds locally (the Buffer, the stream's
spool file, the source file, before a move deletes it) go in with `put()`
under the version just recorded, through the same tmp file, check and
rename as a download. So the read which usually follows a write, a client
syncing a file and the build reading it, is a hit with no GET. It is best effort: a cache error never fails the write, and files
over `max_file_bytes` are skipped, since only `ensureLocal` would find them
and copying every big write would churn the cache. A source changed since its
upload fails the check and is not cached,
and an entry is keyed by its own write's version, so a newer write never
finds it. `stats()` counts hits, misses, bytes, evictions, downloads and
fills.

What it keeps from the disk:

- Paths are normalised by `helper/pathNormalizer`, `..` cannot leave the
  blog's folder, and a name is normalised the way it always has been.
- `resolveCaseInsensitive` picks with `helper/caseSensitivePath.pick`.
- There are no symlinks, so nothing raises `ELOOP` any more, and no file
  modes.

Limits:

- `stat` with `{ hash: true }` (Dropbox's content hash) downloads the file,
  since the index holds only the SHA-1, so a Dropbox reset reads every file
  from S3.
- `listAll` buffers a whole directory.
- Missing files reject with `NotFoundError` (`code: "ENOENT"`), including
  streams, which emit it as `error`.
